we begin with the latest escalation in  the israeli-palestinian conflict gaza's  health ministry says that nine people  have been killed  calm now appears to be prevailing for  the moment this  alive shot from gaza earlier hamas fired  a barrage of rockets from the strip  they were launched in response to what  the group called israeli aggression  in the al-aqsa compound israel is  hitting targets across  gaza with artillery  earlier the al-aqsa mosque compound  islam's third holiest site was the scene  of a violent israeli crackdown these  pictures are from inside the mosque  israeli forces firing rubber-coated  steel bullets tear gas and sound bombs  that palestinian worshipers  and that's the second time that israeli  forces have stormed  the mosque since last week  [Music]  and these pictures are from in and  around the compound which houses the  mosque israeli security forces targeted  palestinian protesters  who were standing guard  the latest numbers from the palestinian  red cross are that more than 330 people  have been  injured speaking earlier israel's prime  minister benjamin netanyahu praised the  security forces for supporting what he  called  a just struggle at upholding law and  order  a struggle is taking place in the heart  of jerusalem this is not a new struggle  this is a struggle between intolerance  and tolerance between law breaking and  violence  and law and order the struggle is not  new because it has transpired over  jerusalem  and the heart of jerusalem for centuries  since the rise of the monotheistic  religions  for years one side ruled and excluded  the two others  and sometimes they changed and the same  thing happened  you can say that in the long history of  jerusalem only under israeli sovereignty  since 1967  we are witnessing a continuous stable  secure period in which we are working to  ensure freedom  of worship and tolerance for all  meanwhile organizers of an israeli march  to commemorate the 1967 occupation of  east jerusalem have cancelled their  rally  there have however been smaller groups  of settlers and right-wing activists  gathering on the streets but the main  march involving thousands of people  has been called off al jazeera's  ibrahim is live in ramallah for us in  the occupied west bank but first  let's speak with harry forces who's  outside the al-aqsa mosque compound  harry  a lot for you to draw together for us  here let's start with  six o'clock local time when as you were  on air  air raid sirens began to sound there in  central israel well that's right and if  we wind the clock back a little bit  before that there was a warning from  hamas's military wing  that if israel did not uh withdraw its  forces from the al-aqsa mosque compound  known as the temple mount to jews if it  didn't withdraw  from sheikh jarrah the neighborhood in  east jerusalem at the center  of this eviction dispute if it didn't  release the people it had detained  during the course of the day then come  six o'clock local time  there would be a price to pay that price  came in the form of  seven rockets that were fired towards  the jerusalem area  one of them was intercepted six fell in  open areas now this is a very  rare event for all of the escalations  and rockets we've seen  out of gaza in recent years uh it's not  thought of it happens in this part of  israel  or rather in the area to the west of  here  which is around the jerusalem area uh  there haven't been rockets targeting  that for several years  so that's a major escalatory step  as far as the israelis are concerned  there will be a response to that  the israeli military is saying that  there will be several days of fighting  in and around gaza they are ruling  nothing out including targeted  assassinations  of leadership uh of they're not ruling  out a ground campaign either  the fact they're saying several days  also carries with it perhaps a  suggestion that they're trying to put  some kind of a time limit on it  but it's very difficult to do that once  these  escalations start because whatever the  israelis do  is likely to bring a response from  inside gaza as well  another thing to tell you about a  serious explosion  deadly one inside northern gaza uh this  is  during the time that there are israeli  airstrikes going on indeed the israeli  military  uh showed a video of a strike from  above saying that it had targeted a cell  a terrorist cell as they called it  a hamas one inside northern gaza  targeting three  uh what they said were terrorists  however there was also  an explosion in beit hanun which killed  nine people according to the palestinian  health ministry including  three children now initially there were  reports that that was  an israeli strike there have also been  sources suggesting that it could have  been a malfunctioning  ordnance of some kind from within gaza  it's extremely unclear as to exactly  what took place there whether the uh the  video that's been tweeted out by the  israeli military  refers to the same incident or not uh  hopefully we will get some kind of  clarity  we are seeking that from the israeli  military to see if they can rule out  that they are responsible for what the  palestinian health ministry says is nine  deaths including three children  and after a day of uh more violence  there in occupied east jerusalem has the  cancellation of this uh this march to  commemorate  the 1967 occupation of east jerusalem  done anything  to diffuse tension  yes it's it's strange really after these  days and days of building pressure and  tension  reaching this boiling point in the  al-aqsa mosque compound earlier  on monday now that we're on the verge  potentially of a very major escalation  between israel  and hamas in gaza the situation here at  damascus gate  on the fringes of the old city is the  calmest that it's been for some time  i think uh that is to some extent  because  of the uh decision to cancel this march  monday was  always worried about as a potentially  very combustible day because  of the march carried out traditionally  on this day  by jewish israelis particularly  religious nationalist and far-right  israelis  celebrating the 1967 capture of east  jerusalem  what they call the reunification what  palestinians consider to be  and international law considers to be  the military occupation that began here  in 1967. if that had been allowed to go  on through the muslim quarter of the old  city  there could have been very serious uh  exchanges of violence between the two  communities that that seemed a very  real possibility up until the last  minute the israeli security  establishment seemed to  go back and forth as to whether they  were going to allow it to take place  in the end at the last minute they  decided no that does represent some  significant  setbacks as far as benjamin netanyahu  and the israeli right wing is concerned  jews were not allowed access onto the  al-aqsa mosque compound the temple mount  on this  important day for them they've uh also  stepped back on this front they've  cancelled  a court hearing that was due to take  place today a supreme court hearing  on the evictions at  just a short distance away from here  and yet we have this potential major  escalation  taking place uh as a result partly  of what we've seen throughout this month  of increased tensions  and of course there was what took place  the very violent scenes that we saw  on the al-aqsa mosque compound itself  earlier on monday  al jazeera's harry fossett there live in  occupied east jerusalem we heard from  israel's prime minister benjamin  netanyahu a short time ago while the  palestinian leadership  has also been reacting to the heightened  attention  let's bring in nida ibrahim is live for  us in  ramallah in the occupied west bank so  anita what  has the palestinian leadership had to  say today  earlier on monday monday the  spokesperson of the palestinian  presidency nabila bourdain has said that  israel  is challenging the u.s administration  that is trying to calm the situation  down  and it's also challenging the  international community and when it  comes to the point of the international  community  we've had a chat with the plo's  executive committee member ahmed  majdalani  who says that the international  community's response and reactions  weren't strong enough to enforce israel  to calm the situation  down and he says that the condemnations  aren't  enough when it comes to that serious  escalation we've also heard from uh  different palestinian officials  including a top  aide to president abbas he tweeted  saying that the palestinian leadership  is going to convene and study  what options they have to confront the  israeli escalation  in occupied east jerusalem but that has  been met with a lot of criticism in the  street  where b people say that they've been  calling  for gaza to respond and they've seen a  response  from the military wings of hamas and  islamic jihad in gaza  while the palestinian authority is still  studying  what to do this is this  these sarcastic remarks we've seen them  a lot on social media  and there is some feeling that the  palestinian authority can do more but  isn't but when it comes to palestinian  officials they will tell you that they  are seeking  a the international community they want  the u.n security council the arab league  and the organization of islamic  cooperation  to deal with the situation and kind of  put pressure  on israel on that regard we've also  heard from  the palestinian prime minister mohammad  who condemned the escalation  let's hear what he had to say in the  beginning of the weekly government  meeting  i hold the israeli government fully  responsible  for what the worshipers were exposed to  in the blessed al-aqsa mosque  and i warn of the consequences that will  result from such incursions  as well as attempts to evacuate the  people of sheikh  shara the original owners of the land  from their homes  i call on the international community to  intervene  immediately to stop these horrific  violations  and to provide protection to the  citizens of the palestinian territories  especially in the city of jerusalem  which is undergoing  ethnic cleansing today jerusalem is  restoring  the glow of the cause and one of the  people  of uh ramallah nida have they been  reacting to all of this  well throughout the past 24 hours and  even before that we've seen  lots of spontaneous protests all across  different cities from the occupied  west bank including protests here in  ramallah where  we've heard people chanting slogans in  support  of those in east jerusalem and sheikh  jarrah  chanting for freedom for palestinians  and for  them not to be evacuated from their  houses but we've also seen many  denouncing the oslo agreement this is  the agreement that has been signed  between the palestinians and israelis or  rather the plo and the israelis that  allowed  for the establishment of the palestinian  authority in the 1990s  and there is a growing feeling among  palestinians that the palestinian  authority isn't doing much  when it comes to their live under  occupation  so we've heard also calls for protests  uh for from palestinians who want uh  these protests to  go to confrontation poets near league  sorry near israeli military checkpoints  and illegal israeli settlements to kind  of confront  with the israeli military because they  they know that  although they can't go to jerusalem it's  surrounded by israeli military  checkpoints they need prior  permits to be able to enter it and some  of them never get it  but they believe that it's important for  them to voice their anger  their cause is the same cause they all  believe that they're suffering under the  israeli occupation  and that's why they want to to have  their voice heard  and they want to voice their anger  al jazeera's nida ibrahim reporting live  from  ramallah in the occupied west bank ada  many thanks indeed

